                subtitle: "The lens changes what you see",
                emoji: "🔍",
                sections: [
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "One Figure, Many Lenses", body: "Jesus of Nazareth might be the most interpreted human being in history. Over 2 billion Christians worship him as God incarnate. 1.8 billion Muslims honor him as one of the greatest prophets. Buddhists admire him as a compassionate teacher. Hindus see him as a guru, saint, or possible avatar. New Age thinkers call him an enlightened master who achieved \"Christ consciousness.\"\n\nSame person. Radically different conclusions.\n\nWhy does this matter for your awakening? Because it reveals something profound about how human consciousness works: we don't see reality directly. We see reality through our framework — our tradition, our conditioning, our inherited lens.\n\nChristians read Isaiah 53 and see Jesus predicted. Jews read the same passage and see the nation of Israel. Muslims read the Gospels and see a prophet whose message was later distorted. New Age thinkers read the same words and see a metaphor for ego death and spiritual rebirth.\n\nThis isn't about who's \"right.\" This is about recognizing that EVERY tradition — including yours — is a lens. And lenses both reveal and distort.\n\nThe goal of this module isn't to convert you to anything. It's to show you how the same figure looks completely different depending on which floor of the building you're standing on (remember the Planes module?). Once you see this pattern with Jesus, you'll start seeing it everywhere — in politics, in science, in your own relationships.\n\nThe person who can hold multiple lenses simultaneously without collapsing into any single one? That's someone who sees clearly. That's Aperture."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Ground Rules", body: "Before we begin:\n\n1. RESPECT: Every tradition described here is someone's deepest truth. We present each view from WITHIN that tradition's own logic, not from outside looking in to criticize.\n\n2. OBJECTIVITY: We're not ranking these views. We're mapping them. Like the Comparison Matrix in Module 5 — different architects, same building.\n\n3. PRIMARY SOURCES: Every claim is traceable to that tradition's own scriptures or scholarly consensus. We're not strawmanning anyone.\n\n4. YOUR DISCERNMENT: After seeing all views, YOU decide what resonates. That's cognitive sovereignty. That's ERSS applied to religion itself.\n\n5. THE PATTERN: Pay attention to what's SHARED across all views, not just what's different. The shared elements are often the signal. The differences are often the noise of cultural conditioning.\n\nLet's begin."),
