            // ═══════════════════════════════════════
            // LESSON 2: Judaism & Islam
            // ═══════════════════════════════════════
            AwakeningLesson(
                id: "ld_m1_l2",
                number: 2,
                title: "Judaism & Islam",
                subtitle: "Teacher vs. prophet — two Abrahamic views",
                emoji: "📖",
                sections: [
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Judaism: A Jewish Teacher, Not the Messiah", body: "In Judaism, Jesus is understood as a first-century Jewish teacher — not the promised Messiah and not divine.\n\nThe key: Judaism's messianic expectations are specific. The Messiah is a future descendant of David who brings visible, lasting peace, restores justice, and gathers the exiles of Israel. Since the world didn't enter that kind of peace in the first century, most Jewish communities don't see Jesus as having fulfilled the messianic job description.\n\nChristians often point to passages like Isaiah 53, Psalm 22, or Daniel 7 as predictions of Jesus. Jewish interpretation reads these texts differently — for example, seeing the \"suffering servant\" in Isaiah as the nation of Israel itself, or as a historical figure from that era.\n\nSo it's not about which verses exist. It's about how they're read. Same text. Different interpretive framework. Different conclusion.\n\nJesus doesn't appear in the Tanakh (Hebrew Bible) itself. References come later in some rabbinic writings, and they're complex, debated, and not central to Jewish faith or practice.\n\nThe heart of Judaism stays focused on: covenant life with God, worshiping the one God (the Shema in Deuteronomy 6:4), keeping Torah, and pursuing justice and mercy.\n\nModern Jewish opinions on Jesus range from skeptical to respectfully curious. Scholars like Martin Buber admired Jesus as a pious Jew. Amy-Jill Levine argues he fits within first-century Judaism's Pharisaic debates.\n\nThe takeaway: Judaism doesn't build its faith around Jesus because it doesn't identify him as Messiah or God. But it recognizes him as part of Jewish and world history."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Islam: One of the Greatest Prophets — But Not Divine", body: "For Muslims, Jesus (known as Isa) is one of the greatest prophets — born of the Virgin Mary, honored with miracles performed by God's permission. The Quran speaks about him with deep respect. He heals, he teaches, he points people back to worshiping the one true God.\n\nIslam even calls Jesus the Messiah (al-Masih), though the meaning differs from Christian theology.\n\nThe main dividing line: divinity. Islam rejects the idea that Jesus is God or the Son of God in the Christian sense. It also rejects the Trinity. From an Islamic viewpoint, God (Allah) is absolutely one — no partners, no shared essence, no God in human form. This is called tawhid.\n\nAnother major difference: the crucifixion. The New Testament presents Jesus's death by crucifixion as a historical event. The Quran says Jesus was not killed or crucified in the way people thought, and that God raised him up (Quran 4:157). Muslims commonly understand this as God saving Jesus from that death.\n\nWhy this matters: In Islam, Jesus is not the final word. He's a signpost pointing forward. The final prophet is Muhammad, and Jesus's role supports that larger storyline.\n\nMany Muslims believe Jesus will return near the end of time to defeat deception and restore justice — not as a crucified savior to be worshiped, but as a servant of God completing his mission.\n\nJesus appears in 93 Quranic verses — more mentions than Muhammad. That's how deeply he's honored. But the framework is fundamentally different: a beloved prophet within a strictly monotheistic theology.\n\nPATTERN TO NOTICE: Both Judaism and Islam share strict monotheism. Both honor Jesus to varying degrees. Both reject the Trinity. Both redirect focus away from Jesus as divine center — Judaism toward Torah, Islam toward Muhammad as final prophet."),
