                number: 3,
                title: "Buddhism & Hinduism",
                subtitle: "Admirable teacher vs. possible avatar",
                emoji: "🕉️",
                sections: [
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Buddhism: Compassionate Teacher, Not Central", body: "Buddhism isn't built around worshiping a creator God or trusting in a savior to remove sin. The core problem is suffering (dukkha), driven by craving and ignorance. The core solution is awakening — seeing reality clearly and living with compassion, mindfulness, and wisdom.\n\nJesus doesn't appear in Buddhist scriptures. You won't find him in the sutras as a doctrinal authority. But in the modern world, many Buddhists talk about Jesus with genuine respect.\n\nSome view him as a wise teacher. Others see parallels between Jesus's compassion and what Buddhism calls the bodhisattva ideal — someone who embodies self-giving love for others, delaying their own final liberation to serve.\n\nThe contrast becomes sharp when you compare claims. In the Gospels, Jesus speaks with unique authority, forgives sins, and calls people into relationship with God. Buddhism doesn't frame the human condition as guilt before God but as bondage to ignorance, healed through insight and practice.\n\nSo when Buddhists engage Jesus, it lands in admiration rather than worship. A moral example, a compassionate healer, an inspirational figure. He can be appreciated without being required.\n\nThe Dalai Lama's \"The Good Heart\" (1996) admires Jesus's compassion but notes doctrinal differences. Thich Nhat Hanh sees parallels in mindfulness practice.\n\nKey distinction: Buddhism's map of the problem and the cure is drawn in a completely different language. No creator God, no sin, no salvation through faith — awakening through direct insight."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Hinduism: Many Paths, Many Views", body: "There isn't a single Hindu view of Jesus because Hinduism is diverse — more like a vast spiritual library than one defined creed.\n\nMany Hindus respect Jesus deeply, seeing him as a holy man, a teacher of love, or a powerful example of devotion (bhakti). Some interpret him through Hindu categories: guru, realized soul, or even avatar (divine incarnation, like Krishna or Rama).\n\nOthers fold Jesus into a \"many paths to God\" framework where he's one true revelation among many — valuable and real, but not exclusive.\n\nThis is where interpretations collide or blend. In the New Testament, Jesus makes strong identity claims and calls for trust and allegiance, especially in John's Gospel (\"I am the way, the truth, and the life\"). Classical Christian teaching centers on Jesus as uniquely God-with-us — not simply one spiritual master alongside others.\n\nAt the same time, Hindu devotional traditions (bhakti) can resonate deeply with Christian devotion — love, surrender, worship. Some Hindus find the Sermon on the Mount compelling. Some admire Jesus's compassion for the poor. Some even incorporate Jesus into personal devotion without formally becoming Christian.\n\nGandhi saw Jesus as a truth-seeker (satyagrahi). Ram Mohan Roy praised his ethical teachings. The variety is the key — two Hindus can talk about Jesus and mean very different things, both speaking sincerely from within their tradition's wide spectrum.\n\nPATTERN TO NOTICE: Both Buddhism and Hinduism can INCLUDE Jesus without placing him at the center. This inclusive approach contrasts with Christianity's exclusive claims. Neither approach is automatically \"right\" — they're different architectures for processing the same data point."),
